{
  "id": "ep085",
  "duration": 50.876,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.229,
      "text": "A giant star in our galaxy regularly chokes on its own soot.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Art Institute of Chicago (CC0)",
        "title": "An Icy Night, New York",
        "credit": "Alfred Stieglitz\nAmerican, 1864\u20131946",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/66258"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 2.533,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 2.533,
          "end": 4.233,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.229,
      "end": 11.949,
      "text": "Discovered in 1795, the supergiant R Coronae Borealis usually shines bright enough for the naked eye.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Corona Borealis (crb).jpg",
        "credit": "IAU and Sky & Telescope",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corona_Borealis_(crb).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.233,
          "end": 8.067,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 8.067,
          "end": 11.933,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 11.949,
      "end": 18.219,
      "text": "But without warning, this \"reverse nova\" can plummet to nearly one-millionth of its normal brightness.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 11.933,
          "end": 18.233,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 18.219,
      "end": 24.764,
      "text": "It doesn't explode outward like a typical star; instead, it suddenly vanishes from view for months.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Corona Borealis (crb).jpg",
        "credit": "IAU and Sky & Telescope",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corona_Borealis_(crb).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 18.233,
          "end": 24.767,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 24.764,
      "end": 30.784,
      "text": "Astronomers found the star is actually smothering itself in a massive cloud of carbon dust.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Porous chondriteIDP.jpg",
        "credit": "The original uploader was Amara at English Wikipedia.",
        "license": "CC BY 1.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porous_chondriteIDP.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 24.767,
          "end": 30.8,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 30.784,
      "end": 37.404,
      "text": "Intense shockwaves force carbon gas to condense into thick, dark soot that blocks all visible light.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Hubble Peers into the Storm (29563971405).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center from Greenbelt, MD, USA",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hubble_Peers_into_the_Storm_(29563971405).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 30.8,
          "end": 34.333,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 34.333,
          "end": 37.4,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 37.404,
      "end": 43.454,
      "text": "While it disappears from our eyes, infrared telescopes can see right through the cosmic smog.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "The dark cloud B68 at different wavelengths (eso9934b).tiff",
        "credit": "ESO",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_dark_cloud_B68_at_different_wavelengths_(eso9934b).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 37.4,
          "end": 40.433,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 40.433,
          "end": 43.467,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 43.454,
      "end": 50.526,
      "text": "Radiation pressure eventually clears the exhaust, revealing why this giant star regularly chokes on its own soot.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Art Institute of Chicago (CC0)",
        "title": "An Icy Night, New York",
        "credit": "Alfred Stieglitz\nAmerican, 1864\u20131946",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/66258"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 43.467,
          "end": 50.867,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
